note: How deep recursion may go is a count of calls, not a question of how much each call needs: up to 1000 calls may
note: be active at once, whatever the function's body looks like.
func depth(number n) do:
    if n is 0 do:
        return 0
    end
    return 1 + depth(n - 1)
end
print(depth(999))

note: The same descent with locals, a loop and nested blocks around the call reaches the same depth.
func heavy(number n) do:
    number result = 0
    list seen = [n]
    if n > 0 do:
        loop each number step in [1] do:
            if step is 1 do:
                result = step + heavy(n - 1)
            end
        end
    end
    return result
end
print(heavy(999))

note: One call more is one too many, and the error is caught like any other.
print(depth(1000)) or_else as Error e do:
    print(e.code, e.message)
end

note: A method counts as a call just the same, and so does making an object of a class that has an init.
class Counter do:
    func down(number n) do:
        if n is 0 do:
            return "bottom"
        end
        return self.down(n - 1)
    end
end
print(Counter().down(999))
print(Counter().down(1000)) or_else as Error e do:
    print(e.code)
end

class Nest do:
    any inner = empty
    func init(number levels) do:
        if levels > 1 do:
            self.inner = Nest(levels - 1)
        end
    end
end
Nest deep = Nest(1000)
print(deep.inner.inner.inner isa Nest)
Nest deeper = Nest(1001) or_else as Error e do:
    print(e.code)
end

note: After the error the count is back where it was: the next call starts from the top.
print(depth(999))
